  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q23-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "23",
    "prompt": "Show that the sum of three consecutive perfect cubes is a multiple of 9.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The sum is $3n(n^2+2)$, which is divisible by 9.\n\nModulo 3, $n(n^2+2)$ is congruent to the product of three consecutive integers.",
      "worked": "Write the cubes as $(n-1)^3$, $n^3$ and $(n+1)^3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(n-1)^3+n^3+(n+1)^3&=3n^3+6n\\\\(n-1)^3+n^3+(n+1)^3&=3n(n^2+2).\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\begin{align*}n(n^2+2)&\\equiv n(n^2-1)\\pmod 3\\\\n(n^2+2)&\\equiv(n-1)n(n+1)\\pmod 3.\\end{align*}$$\nOne of three consecutive integers is divisible by 3. Hence $3\\mid n(n^2+2)$, so $9\\mid3n(n^2+2)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
